WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:03.878
I want to make you a chart

00:00:03.878 --> 00:00:07.090
of how tall people were 
100 years ago.

00:00:09.551 --> 00:00:11.386
Height data tends to be split by gender.

00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:13.513
So, we'll look at women 
for now.

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:15.807
Okay, we're ready.

00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:17.809
We'll chart heights by country.

00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:21.187
Let's look at where my dad's family 
is from: South Korea.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.982
The average woman born near 
the beginning of the 1900s

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:25.316
like my great grandmother

00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:28.570
was just 142 centimeters 
or 4'7".

00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:31.781
For comparison, the average 
American woman 

00:00:31.781 --> 00:00:35.118
was 159 centimeters 
or 5'2".

00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:37.328
If we chart every country...

00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:39.247
we can see that people 
a century ago

00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:42.042
were pretty short for 
today's standards.

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:44.961
But over the next hundred years, 
humans grew.

00:00:45.754 --> 00:00:47.964
Let's just look at those blue bars:

00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:50.008
How much each country grew.

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:52.594
We need to adjust 
the vertical axis

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:54.471
so we can see this chart better.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.058
Ah, that's better.

00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:00.393
Many of the countries that saw 
the most growth

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:03.063
were European, North American 
countries.

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.899
For example, here's France 
and here's the US.

00:01:07.025 --> 00:01:09.402
The countries that grew the least 
were in Africa.

00:01:10.653 --> 00:01:13.364
And Asian countries were 
mostly in the middle.

00:01:14.032 --> 00:01:16.451
But all the way on the right is an outlier.

00:01:16.826 --> 00:01:19.370
A country where women 
grew nearly 8 inches

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:20.497
in the last century.

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:22.957
South Korea.

00:01:22.957 --> 00:01:24.459
And South Korean boys

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:27.295
grew nearly 6 inches 
in that time period.

00:01:28.338 --> 00:01:29.506
So why did humans grow

00:01:29.506 --> 00:01:31.633
so much in such a short amount of time?

00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:34.511
And why did this happen 
in South Korea?

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:40.683
For most of the last 2000 years

00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:42.727
human height didn't change much.

00:01:42.727 --> 00:01:44.771
This chart shows the height 
of European men...

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:47.190
but this stagnation pretty much 
was happening everywhere.

00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:49.818
But in the last 200 years

00:01:49.818 --> 00:01:51.152
we started to see 
some growth.

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:54.030
Here's some better data of 1800s.

00:01:54.572 --> 00:01:56.783
You can see people in wealthier 
European countries 

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:57.826
starting to get taller.

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:01.663
We know a lot about why.

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:04.332
And it all starts with our genes.

00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:07.460
One study from 2006 looked at 
thousands of siblings...

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.296
and analyzed how much of 
their DNA was shared...

00:02:10.505 --> 00:02:13.049
and compared that to how different 
their height was.

00:02:13.508 --> 00:02:15.426
They found that about 80% 
of the difference

00:02:15.426 --> 00:02:17.679
in sibling height is genetic.

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:19.430
But that other 20%

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.432
are external forces that 
affect our height.

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:24.310
Because even if our DNA says

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.688
we can grow to a certain height
 during puberty...

00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:29.315
that growth is 
often interrupted.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:31.109
There's a great study that tracks

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:33.611
English and Welsh boys 
born in the 1890s.

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:36.656
Many of these boys eventually 
enlisted in WWI

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.741
which created a record of their adult height.

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:40.618
Researchers compared that 

00:02:40.618 --> 00:02:42.203
to their childhood living conditions

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:43.663
using the 1901 census.

00:02:44.205 --> 00:02:47.041
They found that, if a person grew up 
in a white collar household...

00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:49.544
which probably had better access to nutrition...

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:50.795
they were on average

00:02:50.795 --> 00:02:52.964
about a half inch taller 
than everyone else.

00:02:53.256 --> 00:02:55.049
If they grew up in a crowded home

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:57.635
where they were more likely 
to spread disease or infection...

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.013
they were nearly a third 
of an inch shorter.

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:02.140
And if they grew up in an industrial area

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.392
exposed to pollution and disease

00:03:04.392 --> 00:03:06.227
they were nearly an inch shorter.

00:03:06.227 --> 00:03:07.812
Even within the same country

00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:10.481
the environment had a noticeable effect 
on people's height.

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:13.151
That's what changed drastically

00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:14.819
in the last 200 years.

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:17.822
Over the last century

00:03:17.822 --> 00:03:21.451
we made massive advancements
 in nutrition, sanitation

00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:23.578
medicine and overall 
quality of life.

00:03:24.078 --> 00:03:26.456
And humans got way taller.

00:03:26.456 --> 00:03:28.625
But now let's put South Korea 
on this chart.

00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:31.586
100 years ago, South Korea was poor

00:03:31.586 --> 00:03:33.838
and the people were shorter 
than the global average.

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:36.549
But by the 1960s, they had 
mostly caught up.

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:39.969
Then something drastic happened.

00:03:39.969 --> 00:03:42.597
A military-led government 
focused on economic growth.

00:03:42.847 --> 00:03:44.307
Eventually, the country shifted

00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:46.059
from producing things like textiles

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:48.436
to consumer electronics and cars.

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:50.980
And South Korean wealth skyrocketed.

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:55.318
In the 60s, many South Koreans 
didn't have enough to eat.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:57.987
The food supply was basically in line

00:03:57.987 --> 00:04:00.156
with other low-income countries.

00:04:00.156 --> 00:04:02.200
But the food supply 
rapidly improved

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.786
and soon it exceeded 
the world average.

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:07.330
And then it caught up to affluent 
countries in Europe.

00:04:08.623 --> 00:04:10.875
As for childhood health, 
in the 1950s

00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:14.170
more than 20% of South Korean kids 
died before age one.

00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:16.422
Worse than most low income countries.

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:19.842
Then, South Korea's infant mortality 
plummeted.

00:04:20.551 --> 00:04:22.595
And as a result of these improved conditions...

00:04:22.804 --> 00:04:26.724
especially for children, South Koreans kept
growing and growing.

00:04:31.991 --> 00:04:34.744
I want to show you 
one more chart.

00:04:34.744 --> 00:04:38.691
This is the height of South Koreans 
and North Koreans in the 1930s.

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:40.969
Back then, they were 
one country

00:04:40.969 --> 00:04:43.258
so naturally they were 
about the same height.

00:04:43.547 --> 00:04:45.549
The North is where my 
maternal grandparents

00:04:45.549 --> 00:04:46.675
and their siblings grew up.

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:49.123
Then in 1945

00:04:49.123 --> 00:04:51.125
the North and South were split up.

00:04:51.125 --> 00:04:53.669
In 1950, the Korean War began.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:55.875
The war ended in a stalemate 

00:04:55.875 --> 00:04:57.502
and North Korea 
shut its borders

00:04:57.502 --> 00:04:59.389
walling itself off from 
the rest of the world.

00:05:00.522 --> 00:05:01.898
In the 1990s

00:05:01.898 --> 00:05:04.316
millions of North Koreans 
starved to death.

00:05:04.717 --> 00:05:06.677
We only have data on 
North Korean height today

00:05:06.677 --> 00:05:08.179
because of the thousands of people

00:05:08.179 --> 00:05:10.556
who escaped to the South 
during that period.

00:05:11.019 --> 00:05:13.063
And in the generations 
since the division...

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:15.399
the height gap between the two Koreas...

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.084
has continuously widened.

00:05:18.408 --> 00:05:19.951
My grandparents were lucky.

00:05:20.152 --> 00:05:22.905
Early in the war, they fled 
their home in North Korea...

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:25.451
and escaped to the southern tip 
of South Korea.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:28.276
And a few years later, 
my mother was born.

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:30.746
My mom is a bit taller 
than her mother...

00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:33.373
and my generation 
is taller than hers.

00:05:34.714 --> 00:05:36.591
Height is inherited

00:05:36.591 --> 00:05:38.912
written into our very genetic code.

00:05:39.523 --> 00:05:41.442
But height is also something...

00:05:42.185 --> 00:05:43.728
history gives to us.

